<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Battle of Cancha Rayada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilean-war-of-independence">Chilean War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Battle of Cancha Rayada was fought on the night of 19 March 1818 near Talca, when royalist forces under Mariano Osorio surprised the camp of the patriot army of San Martín and O'Higgins and scattered it. O'Higgins was wounded, but the patriots regrouped and destroyed the royalist army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maipu">Battle of Maipú</a> two weeks later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-cancha-rayada.jpg" alt="Second Battle of Cancha Rayada" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B1mbo · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_loc_Maule.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In March 1818, during Osorio's campaign in the South American wars of independence, about five thousand royalists under Brigadier Mariano Osorio were concentrated and fortified in Talca. The patriot United Army, about seven thousand strong, was taking up positions on the plains of Cancha Rayada some seven kilometres away, and the Argentine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a>, fearing an attack on his flank, ordered his troops to change position. Osorio, outnumbered and weaker in cavalry, was content to hold Talca, but on the suggestion of Colonel José Ordóñez the royalists decided to attack, with Ordóñez in command.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The attack began at 19:30, when the patriots least expected a battle. Ordóñez went round the city and struck at night behind the patriot vanguard while it was still moving into its new positions, falling on the battalion of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">Bernardo O'Higgins</a> near San Martín's own post. The vanguard scattered; O'Higgins's horse was shot dead and he was wounded in one arm.</p><p>San Martín at first held his ground instead of retreating, and more soldiers fled under fire, leaving weapons and supplies behind, before he ordered a withdrawal. The rear and reserves had no commander, since Colonel Hilarión de la Quintana had gone to headquarters for orders, so Colonel Juan Gregorio de Las Heras took charge and led the retreat, saving what artillery and arms he could. San Martín and O'Higgins were closely pursued by royalist troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By 21 March about three and a half thousand patriots had gathered at San Fernando. Rumours that O'Higgins and San Martín were dead reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> and set off an exodus towards Mendoza, while San Martín reported that he was regrouping and already counted 4,000 men from Curicó to Pelequén. The patriots lost around 150 killed and two hundred prisoners, several hundred deserters and all the Argentine artillery. The royalists also paid heavily, with about two hundred killed, three hundred captured and some six hundred deserted, more than half of the two thousand men who had charged.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Cancha Rayada (1818)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a surprise defeat at <b>Cancha Rayada</b> in March, San Martín and O'Higgins destroyed the main royalist army at the Battle of Maipú on 5 April 1818, securing central Chile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
